course: everyday
lesson: 65
title: A pet is a responsibility
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A pet depends on its family for everything: food, safety, comfort, health and kindness, every day, in good times and bad, for the whole of its life.
explain: Good owners think from the animal's side: a new home, a new baby, a strange house or a vet visit can feel scary to an animal, so they go slowly and calmly.
explain: Small animals in cages and hutches cannot move away from danger, so their owners must do it for them: shade on hot days, shelter and extra bedding on freezing nights.
explain: Owners also keep pets healthy: vet checks and vaccines, flea and worm treatments, and a collar that fits with room for two fingers underneath.
explain: A girl in Vancouver is given a new kitten. She gives it a quiet corner and a few days to settle before lots of cuddles, and when the family's dog meets it, an adult is always watching.
explain: A family in Ohio got a puppy for Christmas, but nobody walked or trained it; it chewed the house, grew bored and anxious, and had to go to a rescue home.
explain: A boy in Leeds forgot to close the guinea pig's run; a fox got in at night and the guinea pig was killed.
fact: Vets check pets' health, give vaccines and find problems early; you keep the pet calm in its carrier and hold it as the vet says.
fact: A new pet needs a quiet place and a few days to settle, because moving house is scary for animals.
fact: A new baby or new pet is introduced slowly and always with an adult watching, because animals can feel jealous or scared.
fact: You ask the owner before touching or feeding a friend's pet, because the owner knows how it behaves.
fact: An animal should never be a surprise gift, because the person may not be able to care for it.
fact: If a family truly cannot keep a pet, they find it a new home through a shelter; abandoning a pet is cruel and against the law in many countries.
fact: A pet that scratches all the time may have fleas, tiny jumping bugs that leave dark specks in the fur and can bite people too.
fact: An adult gives the flea and worm treatment the vet suggests, to keep pets and the family free of parasites.
fact: In play, a pet should catch the toy sometimes, because always losing makes animals stressed or snappy.
fact: Most animals do not like clothes, so a pet is dressed up only if it is calm, and you stop if it struggles.
fact: Small animals in cages can overheat and die quickly, so cages and hutches are kept in the shade.
fact: On freezing nights small animals in hutches need a sheltered place and extra bedding.
fact: A collar fits well when you can slide two fingers under it.
fact: Many pets end up in rescue centres because their owners did not realise how much care they need.
fact: Small pets left in unsecured runs can be caught by foxes, cats, dogs or birds.
exercise: You take your cat to the vet. How can you help? | Keep it calm in its carrier and hold it as the vet says | Let it run around the waiting room | Shout loudly at it to stop it being scared | Hide it under your coat so the vet cannot see it
exercise: Why do pets go to the vet even when they seem well? | Vets give vaccines and find problems early | Vets give pets a bath and a haircut every month | It is the only place pets can eat | Vets teach pets to do tricks and obey commands
exercise: Your new puppy has just arrived home. What is kind? | Give it a quiet place and a few days to settle | Invite all your friends round to hold it at once | Take it to a noisy party on the very first day | Keep picking it up every few minutes
exercise: A new baby is coming to your home, where you have a dog. What is right? | Introduce them slowly, always with an adult watching | Leave the dog alone with the baby so they make friends | Lock the dog outside forever | Let the dog lick the baby's face to say hello to it
exercise: At a friend's house, their dog comes up to you. What should you do before stroking or feeding it? | Ask your friend or their parent | Give it some of your snack first | Stroke it fast before it moves | Call it over with a loud clap of your hands
exercise: You want to give your granny a kitten as a surprise birthday present. What is right? | Never give an animal as a surprise gift | Do it, everyone loves kittens | Do it, but put a big ribbon bow on its neck | Do it, and only tell her about it after a week
exercise: Your family is tired of looking after the rabbit. What is the right thing to do? | Keep caring for it, or find a new home through a shelter | Let it go in the woods so that it can be wild and free | Leave it in its hutch and forget it | Leave it at the park for someone kind to find
exercise: Your dog keeps scratching itself all day. What should you do? | Tell an adult and check for fleas | Give it a long bath in your own shampoo | Ignore it, all dogs scratch | Put socks on its back paws so it stops
exercise: Who should give a pet its worming and flea treatment? | An adult, using the treatment the vet suggests | You, using any medicine in the cupboard | Nobody, pets do not need it | The pet itself, from a bowl of food
exercise: When playing with your cat and a feather toy, what is best? | Let the cat catch the toy sometimes | Never let it catch the toy at all | Hit the cat with the toy | Hold the toy up high where it can never reach it
exercise: You want to dress your dog in a costume. When is this all right? | Only if it is calm and the costume does not restrict it | Always, because dogs love wearing clothes | Only if the costume covers its eyes and ears | Even if it struggles, as it will get used to it in time
exercise: It is a hot sunny day and the guinea pig cage is in the sun. What should you do? | Move it into the shade | Give it a blanket to lie on | Leave it, guinea pigs like heat | Close the door of the cage tighter
exercise: It is going to freeze tonight and the rabbit hutch is in an open yard. What should you do? | Ask an adult to move it to shelter and add bedding | Pour warm water all over the floor of the hutch | Leave it, rabbits have fur | Take away its bedding so it moves about more
exercise: How do you check a pet's collar fits well? | You can slide two fingers under it | It is so tight that nothing fits under it | It is loose enough to slip over its head | It touches the ground when it walks
test: A girl in Belfast teases her kitten with a toy mouse but never lets it catch it. The kitten starts biting her. Why? | Play that always ends in frustration makes animals snappy | Kittens only bite people they dislike | The toy mouse made it hungry, so it tried to eat her fingers | Kittens bite when they are happy and want to say thank you
test: A boy in Auckland sees his cat scratching and finds tiny dark specks in its fur. What should the family do? | Treat it for fleas as the vet suggests | Shave off all its fur at home with clippers | Nothing, specks are normal dirt | Keep the boy away from all cats for good
test: A family in Cardiff brings home a rescue dog. It hides under the table all the first evening. What is best? | Let it settle quietly and give it time | Pull it out so it meets everyone at once | Take it back to the shelter at once | Shout its name over and over until it comes out
test: Why do so many pets given as surprise presents end up in shelters? | The new owners were not ready or able to care for them | Surprise pets are always unhealthy | Shelters ask for surprise pets | Pets dislike wrapping paper and run away
test: A collar is so loose it slips over the dog's head. What could happen? | The dog could slip it off and get lost | The collar will choke the dog when it runs | Nothing, a loose collar is best | The dog's fur will stop growing under it
test: A hamster's cage is put on a sunny windowsill in summer. What is the danger? | It can overheat and die quickly | It will get a suntan | The sun will make it sleep too much | Nothing, hamsters love sunshine
test: A girl visits her friend whose cat looks sweet, and she wants to give it a piece of ham. What is the right first step? | Ask the friend's parent first | Give it quickly while no one is looking | Give it only a small piece | Feed it on the floor and not from her hand
test: A family moves house and is told they cannot take their dog. What is the responsible choice? | Find it a new home through a shelter | Leave it behind in the empty house | Drive it far away and let it out on a quiet road | Tie it to a tree near the new house
test: Why should a new pet and the family's old pet meet slowly with an adult watching? | Animals can feel jealous or scared of newcomers | Old pets always love new pets at once | It saves time on the very first day | New pets need to show they are the boss
test: gonna get my nan a puppy as a surprise for her bday 🎁 | No, a pet should never be a surprise gift | Yes, a puppy is the best surprise | Yes, if you add a nice big bow round its neck | Yes, if the puppy is very small and quiet
test: dogs scratching like mad all week, prob nothing | He may have fleas, tell an adult for treatment | Scratching is normal, ignore it | He is just bored, give him a new chewy toy | He needs more baths with soap every day
test: hows tight should the collar be | You can slide two fingers under it | Tight so you cannot fit a finger | Loose enough to slide off his head | So tight it leaves a mark
test: we cant keep the rabbit anymore, mum says just let him go in the woods | No, find him a new home through a shelter | Yes, rabbits love living in the woods with other wild bunnies | Yes, if you leave some food too | Yes, let him go near a farm
test: forgot to lock the bunny's run, it's just one night | Go and close it now, foxes come at night | Leave it, nothing will happen | Close it in the morning | Put a toy there to scare foxes
test: puppy's chewing everything cos nobody walks him | He needs walks and play, ask the family to share it | Shut him in a small room on his own all day | Give him away tomorrow | Just shout at him really loud every time he chews stuff
test: Ivy says pets look after themselves mostly. true? | No, pets need daily care from their owners | Yes, animals always know exactly what they need | Yes, only babies need care | Yes, unless they are dogs or other big pets
test: heard someone got a puppy for Christmas and by February they dumped it on the motorway | Real lesson: a pet is not a surprise gift, and abandoning a pet is cruel and against the law | Real lesson: puppies are only for summer, and in winter it is fine to let them go wherever you like | It proves Christmas pets are always dumped | It is gossip, so abandoning a pet is fine
test: my mate reckons if a pet's scratching it's just itchy from the weather, no need to do anything | Not quite; constant scratching may mean fleas, so an adult gives the treatment the vet suggests | He is right, weather makes pets itch, so it goes away by itself when the season changes, no fuss | He is right, scratching is always normal | He is right, bath it in cold water
test: Ellie's family gave their dog to a shelter and people say they're heartless, pass it on | Do not pass it on; finding a pet a new home through a shelter is the responsible choice | Pass it on, because anyone who gives up their dog is heartless and everybody should know about it | Pass it on, so nobody trusts them | Pass it on, and say the dog cried
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a new pet's arrival in a home? | It requires a quiet place and several days in which to settle | It should be shown to all neighbours at once | It should be handled continuously from arrival | It requires no special arrangements
exam: Identify the reason for which an animal ought never to be given as a surprise gift. | The recipient may be unable to care for it | Animals are distressed by wrapping paper and ribbons | Gifts must be inanimate by law | Surprise animals cost considerably more to purchase
exam: It being given that a family can no longer keep its pet, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To find it a new home through a shelter | To release it in a park | To leave it outside a shop where people pass | To keep it locked in a shed for the remainder of its life
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning play with a pet. | The pet should sometimes catch the toy, since always losing causes stress | The pet should never be allowed to win | The pet should be teased until it gives up | The game should always end with a punishment
